<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Instruction of Vladimir Monomakh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Testament of Vladimir Monomakh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Instruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a> is a work written by the grand prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> Vladimir Monomakh, probably in the early twelfth century, as guidance for his sons. It combines advice on the duties of a Christian ruler, such as justice, mercy and keeping one's oath, with an autobiographical account of his many campaigns and hunts. It survives only in the Laurentian copy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a>, made in 1377.</p></div>
